00:05:34.701 --> 00:05:35.200
So--

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:36.410
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
but before we do that,

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:38.034
you want to tell us
a little about what

00:05:38.034 --> 00:05:40.070
you do at Home Depot,
what's your role there,

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:41.195
and what does that involve?

00:05:41.195 --> 00:05:42.111
WILLIAM BONNELL: Sure.

00:05:42.111 --> 00:05:44.320
So I'm responsible for the
reliability engineering

00:05:44.320 --> 00:05:46.420
strategy The Home Depot.

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:49.330
So that's the broad across
the entire enterprise.

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:53.050
My specific responsibilities
in terms of teams that I manage

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:55.390
are our e-commerce-- so
that's the customer facing

00:05:55.390 --> 00:05:57.100
the e-commerce part
of the business--

00:05:57.100 --> 00:05:58.960
and then our back end order
management, so everything

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:00.430
we're doing with
order management.

00:06:00.430 --> 00:06:02.590
We're talking
millions of customers

00:06:02.590 --> 00:06:03.760
per day come to the website.

00:06:03.760 --> 00:06:06.577
And we're processing millions
of transactions and orders

00:06:06.577 --> 00:06:07.660
on the back end every day.

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:10.284
So I'm looking after
that every day.

00:06:10.284 --> 00:06:11.200
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:15.830
So two things-- first one is I
noticed that we say Home Depot,

00:06:15.830 --> 00:06:17.090
and you say it the Home Depot.

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:18.330
What is the correct
way of saying this?

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:19.080
MARK MANDEL: The Home Depot is--

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:19.610
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
The Home Depot--

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:20.754
MARK MANDEL: Got it,

00:06:20.754 --> 00:06:23.170
WILLIAM BONNELL: We actually
call ourselves THD, acronym--

00:06:23.170 --> 00:06:24.700
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It
is like the internet.

00:06:24.700 --> 00:06:25.783
MARK MANDEL: Got it, yeah.

00:06:25.783 --> 00:06:28.330
WILLIAM BONNELL: We're
the Ohio State University.

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:30.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And
second question is--

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:32.140
what does your stack look like?

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:33.700
What are-- like
you said, did you

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:36.400
process a bunch
of events per day?

00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:37.990
How do you process those?

00:06:37.990 --> 00:06:38.875
Are you on premise?

00:06:38.875 --> 00:06:40.055
Are you on the Cloud?

00:06:40.055 --> 00:06:41.680
WILLIAM BONNELL:
Well, right now, we're

00:06:41.680 --> 00:06:43.010
in the middle of a hybrid.

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:45.250
We have on-premises,
and we have cloud.

00:06:45.250 --> 00:06:47.710
We've actually been working
with Google for about two years

00:06:47.710 --> 00:06:51.010
to move our e-commerce platform
to the Google Cloud platform.

00:06:51.010 --> 00:06:53.430
So we're a little bit
in a hybrid today.

00:06:53.430 --> 00:06:54.680
We actually talked about that.

00:06:54.680 --> 00:06:57.190
We had a couple of
speaking sessions

00:06:57.190 --> 00:06:59.350
this conference
where we actually

00:06:59.350 --> 00:07:01.030
talked about that architecture.

00:07:01.030 --> 00:07:04.620
So we're about 75% into the
Google Cloud platform now,

00:07:04.620 --> 00:07:06.350
and we're finishing
it up this year.

00:07:06.350 --> 00:07:08.100
MARK MANDEL: So that's
really interesting.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:09.670
So can we get some
history there?

00:07:09.670 --> 00:07:11.505
It sounds like you were
originally on-prem,

00:07:11.505 --> 00:07:14.327
and then at some point, you
made the decision to shift.

00:07:14.327 --> 00:07:15.160
What happened there?

00:07:15.160 --> 00:07:16.300
Why did you make that decision?

00:07:16.300 --> 00:07:17.216
WILLIAM BONNELL: Sure.

00:07:17.216 --> 00:07:21.040
So we had a classic
legacy e-commerce platform

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:22.810
that we've been
running for many years.

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:26.170
And that really got to the
point where it served us well

00:07:26.170 --> 00:07:26.830
for many years.

00:07:26.830 --> 00:07:29.038
But it got to the point
where it was constraining us.

00:07:29.038 --> 00:07:32.290
It was constraining us both
from infrastructure scalability

00:07:32.290 --> 00:07:34.610
standpoint-- very hard
to kind of scale it out.

00:07:34.610 --> 00:07:36.520
Plus, we had a big,
monolithic database

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:40.050
that was almost
impossible to scale out

00:07:40.050 --> 00:07:41.950
at our level of scale.

00:07:41.950 --> 00:07:45.190
Imagine running a $5
plus billion website

00:07:45.190 --> 00:07:47.399
on pretty much a
single database.

00:07:47.399 --> 00:07:48.940
And of course, you
can scale that up,

00:07:48.940 --> 00:07:50.420
and you can do
some things there.

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:51.628
But that was constraining us.

00:07:51.628 --> 00:07:54.100
But in addition to that,
the velocity of our business

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:54.970
was constrained.

00:07:54.970 --> 00:07:58.420
The throughput of being able
to deploy constant innovation

00:07:58.420 --> 00:08:01.570
to it was constrained with the
type of platform we were on.

00:08:04.462 --> 00:08:05.920
First and foremost,
we were looking

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:08.980
for building a custom
platform that will allow

00:08:08.980 --> 00:08:11.129
us to innovate much faster.

00:08:11.129 --> 00:08:13.420
We had gotten to the point
where the old platform where

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:16.497
we could deploy weekly, that
was as good as we could do

00:08:16.497 --> 00:08:17.330
was a weekly deploy.

00:08:17.330 --> 00:08:18.370
MARK MANDEL: That
was pretty good.

00:08:18.370 --> 00:08:19.390
WILLIAM BONNELL: And we
wanted to get the point where

00:08:19.390 --> 00:08:20.890
actually-- and we're
doing that now.

00:08:20.890 --> 00:08:23.000
We're deploying
many times a day,

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:25.560
many different things every day.

00:08:25.560 --> 00:08:27.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:08:27.080 --> 00:08:27.580
So yeah.

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:29.538
You gave us a little bit
of the reasons why you

00:08:29.538 --> 00:08:31.300
decided to move to the cloud.

00:08:31.300 --> 00:08:33.140
How did you move to the cloud?

00:08:33.140 --> 00:08:35.530
WILLIAM BONNELL: So
we moved to the cloud,

00:08:35.530 --> 00:08:38.679
chipping away one
piece at a time.

00:08:38.679 --> 00:08:40.520
Even before we started
working with Google,

00:08:40.520 --> 00:08:43.179
we actually started
pulling out some

00:08:43.179 --> 00:08:46.180
of the pieces of the
e-commerce platform

00:08:46.180 --> 00:08:49.122
into a microservices-like
architecture.

00:08:49.122 --> 00:08:51.580
So we might pull out something
like the product information

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:54.712
page on the site, or it could
be the promotions engine,

00:08:54.712 --> 00:08:55.420
things like that.

00:08:55.420 --> 00:08:57.044
We started pulling
some of those pieces

00:08:57.044 --> 00:08:59.920
out into our
on-premise environment,

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:01.544
even before we
started with Google.

00:09:01.544 --> 00:09:03.210
And then we continue
with that strategy.

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:06.160
So we say one of the first
things we launched into Google

00:09:06.160 --> 00:09:07.090
was our store finder.

00:09:07.090 --> 00:09:10.390
So that thing about store finder
is a service, self-contained.

00:09:10.390 --> 00:09:12.160
We can build a development
team around it.

00:09:12.160 --> 00:09:14.230
They can innovate and push
as often as they want,

00:09:14.230 --> 00:09:15.729
and they don't have
to go coordinate

00:09:15.729 --> 00:09:18.310
with the team that's doing
the cart in our old platform.

00:09:18.310 --> 00:09:20.282
The cart team and
the store finder team

00:09:20.282 --> 00:09:21.740
had to push their
changes together.

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:23.620
Didn't really make a
whole lot of sense,

00:09:23.620 --> 00:09:26.410
but that's the way our
deployment architecture worked.

00:09:26.410 --> 00:09:29.440
MARK MANDEL: So actually, we
get this question all the time

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:31.180
when people like,
I had this thing,

00:09:31.180 --> 00:09:32.710
and I want to move
it to the cloud.

00:09:32.710 --> 00:09:35.830
How did you choose which
pieces to move when,

00:09:35.830 --> 00:09:37.330
or what part to take out?

00:09:37.330 --> 00:09:38.992
Was there a safety
consideration,

00:09:38.992 --> 00:09:40.450
or you wanted to
get a new feature?

00:09:40.450 --> 00:09:41.824
How did you make
those decisions?

00:09:41.824 --> 00:09:44.140
WILLIAM BONNELL: Yeah,
that's kind of challenging.

00:09:44.140 --> 00:09:46.090
It really depends on--

00:09:46.090 --> 00:09:47.507
there's a number
of factors there.

00:09:47.507 --> 00:09:48.881
One of the things
that we've been

00:09:48.881 --> 00:09:50.405
doing throughout
this migration--

00:09:50.405 --> 00:09:52.330
and it's taken us the
last couple of years--

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:55.150
is still using our on-prem
e-commerce platform

00:09:55.150 --> 00:09:56.130
as the nucleus.

00:09:56.130 --> 00:09:57.320
So that's the nucleus.

00:09:57.320 --> 00:09:59.620
And as we're pulling
pieces out, we're

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:03.110
still running things
through that central hub,

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:05.740
and so that I can make calls
out to services, for example,

00:10:05.740 --> 00:10:08.664
that we may launch into
the Google Cloud platform.

00:10:08.664 --> 00:10:10.330
So we've just been
doing that piecemeal.

00:10:10.330 --> 00:10:11.890
The other thing
that we did was we

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:14.223
did some of the things that
may be a little easier, less

00:10:14.223 --> 00:10:15.400
transactional initially.

00:10:15.400 --> 00:10:16.840
I mentioned the store finder.

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:18.220
That's something that's
a service that doesn't

00:10:18.220 --> 00:10:19.480
have a transactional nature.

00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:21.927
It's more of a read
only type of service.

00:10:21.927 --> 00:10:24.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, I think
that is worth mentioning.

00:10:24.260 --> 00:10:26.093
There's a book that
I've been reading about,

00:10:26.093 --> 00:10:27.190
specifically, this topic.

00:10:27.190 --> 00:10:29.937
The question you asked,
there's a whole book on it.

00:10:29.937 --> 00:10:32.020
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I'm
sure there's more than one.

00:10:32.020 --> 00:10:32.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:10:32.670 --> 00:10:33.439
There's a book.

00:10:33.439 --> 00:10:34.480
I think it's really good.

00:10:34.480 --> 00:10:35.560
WILLIAM BONNELL: Well,
there's a microservices book,

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:36.670
which is the O'Reilly book.

00:10:36.670 --> 00:10:38.336
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Exactly, by Sam Newman.

00:10:38.336 --> 00:10:39.600
That's exactly what I had had.

00:10:39.600 --> 00:10:40.360
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice.

00:10:40.360 --> 00:10:41.230
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It
is a very good book,

00:10:41.230 --> 00:10:42.890
exactly covering those topics.

00:10:42.890 --> 00:10:45.310
So if anyone in the audience
is curious about it,

00:10:45.310 --> 00:10:46.394
I definitely recommend it.

00:10:46.394 --> 00:10:47.309
WILLIAM BONNELL: Yeah.

00:10:47.309 --> 00:10:48.520
And that's actually a book--

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:50.316
I know specifically,
it was one that we

00:10:50.316 --> 00:10:51.940
had talked a lot
about internally, even

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:53.750
before we went on this journey.

00:10:53.750 --> 00:10:56.410
There's another one also, and
the name is slipping my mind.

00:10:56.410 --> 00:10:58.535
But it's an O'Reilly book--
just came out recently.

00:10:58.535 --> 00:11:00.940
It was written by
an SRE at Uber.

00:11:00.940 --> 00:11:04.090
But it's about running
microservices, operating

00:11:04.090 --> 00:11:05.264
microservices in production.

00:11:05.264 --> 00:11:06.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:08.290
Since we're talking
about books, have

00:11:08.290 --> 00:11:10.766
you read the SRE book,
the Google SRE book?

00:11:10.766 --> 00:11:11.890
What do you think about it?

00:11:11.890 --> 00:11:12.820
WILLIAM BONNELL:
Yeah, most definitely

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:14.440
read the Google SRE book--

00:11:14.440 --> 00:11:16.840
excellent book, great theme.

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:20.080
I really like the approach
where they chose real SREs

00:11:20.080 --> 00:11:21.770
within Google to
actually write chapters

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:23.080
that they're experts in.

00:11:23.080 --> 00:11:24.742
That really worked well.

00:11:24.742 --> 00:11:26.200
I've read the book--
actually, have

00:11:26.200 --> 00:11:28.060
a Ben Trainer-signed
copy of the book, which

00:11:28.060 --> 00:11:28.870
I'm very excited about.

00:11:28.870 --> 00:11:29.550
MARK MANDEL: Very nice.

00:11:29.550 --> 00:11:31.320
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That is pretty fancy.

00:11:31.320 --> 00:11:32.945
MARK MANDEL: So now
I'm curious though.

00:11:32.945 --> 00:11:34.870
You're director of
SRE at The Home Depot.

00:11:34.870 --> 00:11:35.260
WILLIAM BONNELL: The Home Depot.

00:11:35.260 --> 00:11:36.746
FRANCESC CAMPOY: The Home Depot.

00:11:36.746 --> 00:11:38.620
MARK MANDEL: Do you see
any major differences

00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:41.770
in terms of how
you do SRE at THD,

00:11:41.770 --> 00:11:46.130
or versus what you've seen, at
least externally from Google?

00:11:46.130 --> 00:11:47.490
How's that set up for you?

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:48.365
WILLIAM BONNELL: Yep.

00:11:48.365 --> 00:11:51.162
Well, I'll mention it really
isn't external from Google

00:11:51.162 --> 00:11:52.870
in the sense that
we've been very engaged

00:11:52.870 --> 00:11:55.570
with Google's Customer
Reliability Engineering Team.

00:11:55.570 --> 00:11:57.220
We've been heavily
engaged with them.

00:11:57.220 --> 00:12:03.450
And so they've helped inform us
on Google's way of doing SRE.

00:12:03.450 --> 00:12:05.860
A couple of things I
would mention there is,

00:12:05.860 --> 00:12:08.560
the historical context
that The Home Depot,

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:10.760
and our e-commerce team is--

00:12:10.760 --> 00:12:15.010
we historically had more of what
you might call web engineering

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:16.690
or web operations
team, where we're

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:19.230
managing the infrastructure.

00:12:19.230 --> 00:12:23.560
It's more manual, We do have
some automation in there.

00:12:23.560 --> 00:12:26.500
But the infrastructure
itself is not manual,

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:28.480
and the provisioning
is much harder.

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:32.335
So as we evolved
into the SRE model,

00:12:32.335 --> 00:12:34.960
we did that at the same time as
we migrated to the Google Cloud

00:12:34.960 --> 00:12:35.950
platform.

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:37.750
And the big difference
there is we're

00:12:37.750 --> 00:12:39.910
interacting with the
infrastructure everything,

00:12:39.910 --> 00:12:41.890
firewall, compute, storage.

00:12:41.890 --> 00:12:44.500
You name it, we're interacting
with it through APIs,

00:12:44.500 --> 00:12:46.490
and we're provisioning
things automatically.

00:12:46.490 --> 00:12:50.110
So we're no longer logging into
servers and making changes.

00:12:50.110 --> 00:12:52.510
We're doing everything
in an automated way.

00:12:52.510 --> 00:12:54.130
That's been really
important to us.

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:55.750
MARK MANDEL: No longer
picking up racks of servers

00:12:55.750 --> 00:12:56.790
and physical
firewalls and stuff?

00:12:56.790 --> 00:12:57.440
WILLIAM BONNELL: No, not at all.

00:12:57.440 --> 00:12:58.990
In fact, that's a
distant memory for us.

00:12:58.990 --> 00:13:01.090
And it's just something
we don't even think about.

00:13:01.090 --> 00:13:03.760
So that's a force
multiplier for us.

00:13:03.760 --> 00:13:06.760
So rather than spending those
calories on that type of work,

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:09.670
we're spending the calories
on automating our platform.

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:10.961
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That is great.

00:13:10.961 --> 00:13:13.870
So you mentioned that
you know SRE culture too

00:13:13.870 --> 00:13:16.357
because you were
interacting with CRE?

00:13:16.357 --> 00:13:18.190
Could you tell us more
about your experience

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:19.106
interacting with them?

00:13:19.106 --> 00:13:22.090
WILLIAM BONNELL: Sure,
so Dave Renson, who

00:13:22.090 --> 00:13:25.030
invented the idea of CRE,
created the idea of CRE

00:13:25.030 --> 00:13:26.170
within Google--

00:13:26.170 --> 00:13:33.130
and Luke Stone approached me
in probably the summertime

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:34.840
last year with this idea.

00:13:34.840 --> 00:13:40.000
And this idea was bringing
SRE principles to customers,

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:41.670
and wondered if
I'd be interested.

00:13:41.670 --> 00:13:44.020
And before they could finish
asking me the question,

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:44.650
I said yes.

00:13:44.650 --> 00:13:47.470
I would definitely be
interested in that.

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:50.499
And one of the things I
think a lot about when

00:13:50.499 --> 00:13:52.540
I'm thinking about public
cloud, and I'm thinking

00:13:52.540 --> 00:13:54.880
about enterprise is
considering public cloud,

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:56.970
there's a couple of
big considerations

00:13:56.970 --> 00:13:58.207
for an enterprise.

00:13:58.207 --> 00:14:00.040
One of them is, of
course, going to be cost.

00:14:00.040 --> 00:14:03.490
And so thinking about how
to manage that properly

00:14:03.490 --> 00:14:05.224
in the cloud is important.

00:14:05.224 --> 00:14:06.640
But the second one
is reliability,

00:14:06.640 --> 00:14:07.700
because if you
have an enterprise,

00:14:07.700 --> 00:14:09.640
and they're a little nervous
about going to the cloud,

00:14:09.640 --> 00:14:11.710
and they start pushing
mission-critical workloads

00:14:11.710 --> 00:14:14.080
out there, and they
fail, regardless

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:16.480
of whether it's the cloud
provider's fault or not,

00:14:16.480 --> 00:14:18.940
they're going to
potentially pull back.

00:14:18.940 --> 00:14:22.750
And so the idea of
CRE is to, especially

00:14:22.750 --> 00:14:25.690
for very large customers,
is to help them build out

00:14:25.690 --> 00:14:28.780
the practices of reliability
so that they're actually

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:30.790
successful when they
deploy their workloads out

00:14:30.790 --> 00:14:31.630
to the cloud.

00:14:31.630 --> 00:14:32.780
It's not trivial.

00:14:32.780 --> 00:14:34.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So you've
been working with CRE

00:14:34.780 --> 00:14:36.790
for quite a while, I imagine.

00:14:36.790 --> 00:14:41.110
Is there any moment that you
think it as fun or interesting

00:14:41.110 --> 00:14:42.430
that you could share?

00:14:42.430 --> 00:14:44.430
WILLIAM BONNELL: Well,
there's actually probably

00:14:44.430 --> 00:14:45.850
a lot of moments.

00:14:45.850 --> 00:14:49.180
First of all, I'll say the
team, AJ, and Matt Brown,

00:14:49.180 --> 00:14:53.151
Adrian Hilton, Luke Stone, Dave
Renson, this really crack team

00:14:53.151 --> 00:14:53.650
of--

00:14:53.650 --> 00:14:57.070
I mean, this is some
really talented SREs.

00:14:57.070 --> 00:14:59.050
And then one of topics
that we explored,

00:14:59.050 --> 00:15:03.025
even in the happy hour with them
with the bends the other day

00:15:03.025 --> 00:15:06.409
was the software engineer
versus reliability engineer.

00:15:06.409 --> 00:15:08.950
How do you get the talent into
reliability engineering teams?

00:15:08.950 --> 00:15:12.370
It's clear that Google hires
the best talent in the world

00:15:12.370 --> 00:15:15.190
into their SRE teams.

00:15:15.190 --> 00:15:17.680
And so these guys
definitely demonstrated

00:15:17.680 --> 00:15:21.340
a very deep understanding of how
to run large-scale production

00:15:21.340 --> 00:15:22.660
systems.

00:15:22.660 --> 00:15:26.950
And so the team was really,
really, really insightful.

00:15:26.950 --> 00:15:29.440
They did googly
things, SRE things,

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:31.600
like application
reliability reviews,

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:32.939
and they did deep dive.

00:15:32.939 --> 00:15:34.480
Actually, one of
the things we talked

00:15:34.480 --> 00:15:36.820
about in the very beginning
of the conversation

00:15:36.820 --> 00:15:39.070
with Dave and Luke
was, if they were

00:15:39.070 --> 00:15:40.870
going to do this, and
share fate with us,

00:15:40.870 --> 00:15:42.910
and be in our Black
Friday war room,

00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:48.340
they wanted us to remediate
any high-risk items they found.

00:15:48.340 --> 00:15:51.180
So we agreed to that,
and we did that.

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:53.680
MARK MANDEL: So actually, you
bring up an interesting point.

00:15:53.680 --> 00:15:55.450
So I used to do a lot
of e-commerce stuff.

00:15:55.450 --> 00:15:58.650
Black Friday is a very big
time for a lot of people

00:15:58.650 --> 00:15:59.650
who do e-commerce stuff.

00:15:59.650 --> 00:16:00.890
How was your Black Friday?

00:16:00.890 --> 00:16:01.500
WILLIAM BONNELL: Yeah.

00:16:01.500 --> 00:16:02.791
No, our Black Friday was great.

00:16:02.791 --> 00:16:06.790
And, in fact, Paul Gaffney,
our SVP of development,

00:16:06.790 --> 00:16:09.190
mentioned that in the keynote,
that we actually had--

00:16:09.190 --> 00:16:11.590
this last Black Friday
we had half of our site

00:16:11.590 --> 00:16:14.122
in on-prem and half of our
site on Google Cloud platform--

00:16:14.122 --> 00:16:16.330
so a lot of risk even there,
you think about not even

00:16:16.330 --> 00:16:18.070
just in the Google
Cloud platform,

00:16:18.070 --> 00:16:19.660
but the interconnectivity
and so forth

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:21.470
with our on-premise platform.

00:16:21.470 --> 00:16:23.803
And so we talked a little bit
about that in the keynote.

00:16:23.803 --> 00:16:26.680
But just to cut to the chase,
it was a very successful Black

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:30.260
Friday, 100% availability,
performance was rock solid,

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:32.260
and the Google CRE team
was right there with us.

00:16:32.260 --> 00:16:34.385
They were right there in
a virtual war room with us

00:16:34.385 --> 00:16:35.200
the whole time.

00:16:35.200 --> 00:16:36.370
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Well,
congratulations on that.

00:16:36.370 --> 00:16:37.517
I know it's not easy, so--

00:16:37.517 --> 00:16:38.350
WILLIAM BONNELL: No.

00:16:38.350 --> 00:16:39.220
Nope, it's not.

00:16:39.220 --> 00:16:41.220
And it's getting
harder and harder.

00:16:41.220 --> 00:16:44.860
With the type of architecture,
the microservices architecture,

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:47.710
and the amount of change we have
is getting harder and harder

00:16:47.710 --> 00:16:48.402
every year.

00:16:48.402 --> 00:16:49.360
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Great.

00:16:49.360 --> 00:16:52.750
So I'm curious about
how you handle that

00:16:52.750 --> 00:16:56.720
Black Friday, meaning what
is the stack behind it?

00:16:56.720 --> 00:16:58.519
Are you running
on compute engine?

00:16:58.519 --> 00:16:59.310
What are you doing?

00:16:59.310 --> 00:16:59.390
WILLIAM BONNELL: Yeah.

00:16:59.390 --> 00:17:01.390
One thing I'll mention
is probably interesting--

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:03.860
and it actually came up
in my talk on Wednesday--

00:17:03.860 --> 00:17:07.150
is we didn't have to provision
servers six months in advance

00:17:07.150 --> 00:17:10.180
only to have them sit around
for the remainder of the year.

00:17:10.180 --> 00:17:12.464
So we, actually, at
CRE's recommendation,

00:17:12.464 --> 00:17:13.839
we actually
increased our quotas,

00:17:13.839 --> 00:17:17.410
and actually bumped up
our capacity about a week

00:17:17.410 --> 00:17:19.030
before Black Friday
just in case.

00:17:19.030 --> 00:17:22.459
If we lost a region, we
wouldn't have any blips there.

00:17:22.459 --> 00:17:24.250
And then after we got
through Cyber Monday,

00:17:24.250 --> 00:17:25.839
we just provisioned
that back down.

00:17:25.839 --> 00:17:28.300
So right there, the
power of the cloud,

00:17:28.300 --> 00:17:30.280
the elasticity of
the cloud right

00:17:30.280 --> 00:17:33.047
then and there is
a huge deal for us.

00:17:33.047 --> 00:17:35.380
In terms of platform-- so
we've been working with Google

00:17:35.380 --> 00:17:37.050
for about two years now.

00:17:37.050 --> 00:17:38.860
So some of the
choices we made were

00:17:38.860 --> 00:17:42.710
architecturally the right choice
at the time two years ago.

00:17:42.710 --> 00:17:44.740
So we're very much
on compute engine.

00:17:44.740 --> 00:17:46.930
We're not running in
containers at this point,

00:17:46.930 --> 00:17:50.620
but that's mostly just because
it wasn't mature at the time

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:51.879
when we started doing this.

00:17:51.879 --> 00:17:53.420
MARK MANDEL: Managed
instance groups?

00:17:53.420 --> 00:17:53.590
WILLIAM BONNELL: Oh, yeah.

00:17:53.590 --> 00:17:55.548
We're definitely using
managed instance groups.

00:17:55.548 --> 00:17:57.250
We're running out
of multiple regions.

00:17:57.250 --> 00:17:59.080
In each region, we
have multiple zones.

00:17:59.080 --> 00:18:00.060
We actually took
this opportunity

00:18:00.060 --> 00:18:01.518
when we were building
our platform.

00:18:01.518 --> 00:18:03.910
We actually took the
opportunity from the ground up

00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:06.026
to build every
application hot hot.

00:18:06.026 --> 00:18:07.400
So everything we
have is hot hot.

00:18:07.400 --> 00:18:09.140
We actually just did a--

00:18:09.140 --> 00:18:10.560
and I call it a DR test.

00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:12.310
So it doesn't feel
like a DR test anymore.

00:18:12.310 --> 00:18:14.050
But we just recently
did a DR test

00:18:14.050 --> 00:18:16.960
where we basically just shut all
the traffic off to the US East

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:19.030
just to see how
that would behave.

00:18:19.030 --> 00:18:21.700
And all of our workloads
were on hot hot.

00:18:21.700 --> 00:18:23.800
So it was a nonevent for us.

00:18:23.800 --> 00:18:25.122
That's pretty huge for us.

00:18:25.122 --> 00:18:26.830
MARK MANDEL: So you
use the term hot hot.

00:18:26.830 --> 00:18:28.380
For those people
who aren't familiar,

00:18:28.380 --> 00:18:29.020
what do you mean by that?

00:18:29.020 --> 00:18:30.769
WILLIAM BONNELL: Right,
so active active--

00:18:30.769 --> 00:18:32.560
you have an application.

00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:34.960
In the easiest case,
where you don't have a--

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:35.980
it's not transactional.

00:18:35.980 --> 00:18:38.820
That's just deploying it
to multiple-- in my case,

00:18:38.820 --> 00:18:42.190
it would be multiple
data centers,

00:18:42.190 --> 00:18:44.217
multiple regions, ideally.

00:18:44.217 --> 00:18:46.300
In the case where you have
a transactional system,

00:18:46.300 --> 00:18:46.990
you have the data.

00:18:46.990 --> 00:18:49.239
You have your back end data
that's actually replicated

00:18:49.239 --> 00:18:51.440
across those regions.

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:52.190
MARK MANDEL: Cool.

00:18:52.190 --> 00:18:54.110
And you were also
talking about before you

00:18:54.110 --> 00:18:56.340
were hitting certain scale
limits with your database.

00:18:56.340 --> 00:18:58.720
Have you actually started moving
data into Google Cloud as well,

00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:00.240
and splitting that
out per service?

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:00.730
WILLIAM BONNELL: Oh,
yeah, definitely.

00:19:00.730 --> 00:19:00.940
Yeah.

00:19:00.940 --> 00:19:02.530
So every microservice,
if they need a database,

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:03.550
they have a database.

00:19:03.550 --> 00:19:08.724
We generally-- not every service
we have needs a database.

00:19:08.724 --> 00:19:10.390
Sometimes it could
be a search platform,

00:19:10.390 --> 00:19:11.306
those sorts of things.

00:19:11.306 --> 00:19:13.350
But everyone that
needs a database-- that

00:19:13.350 --> 00:19:15.610
was a big principle for us,
because in the old world

00:19:15.610 --> 00:19:17.440
everything ran against
the same database,

00:19:17.440 --> 00:19:21.162
and that created
some constraints.

00:19:21.162 --> 00:19:22.870
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We
talk about databases.

00:19:22.870 --> 00:19:25.480
Can you talk a little bit about
how do you store your data?

00:19:25.480 --> 00:19:26.396
WILLIAM BONNELL: Sure.

00:19:26.396 --> 00:19:30.370
So in our case, our primary
database is Casandra.

00:19:30.370 --> 00:19:32.537
We actually decided to
self-manage our own database.

00:19:32.537 --> 00:19:33.911
That's something--
we're actually

00:19:33.911 --> 00:19:36.400
looking at the database
platforms that are available,

00:19:36.400 --> 00:19:38.464
Cloud SQL, Cloud
Datastore, even Spanner.

00:19:38.464 --> 00:19:39.880
Spanner is actually
very exciting.

00:19:39.880 --> 00:19:42.010
If you think about--

00:19:42.010 --> 00:19:43.810
we're a top 10
internet retailer.

00:19:43.810 --> 00:19:46.870
And you think about our cart
service and check-out service.

00:19:46.870 --> 00:19:49.030
Something like Spanner
would be very attractive.

00:19:49.030 --> 00:19:51.460
Of course, that's
just in beta now.

00:19:51.460 --> 00:19:53.650
It wasn't even
something we knew about

00:19:53.650 --> 00:19:56.700
other than the white paper,
and CockroachDB, and so forth.

00:19:56.700 --> 00:19:57.590
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Basically,
it came out last week.

00:19:57.590 --> 00:19:58.215
So it's normal.

00:19:58.215 --> 00:19:59.980
You have not been
[INAUDIBLE] yet.

00:19:59.980 --> 00:20:01.690
WILLIAM BONNELL: So we decided
to manage our own Casandra.

00:20:01.690 --> 00:20:02.481
And that has been--

00:20:02.481 --> 00:20:04.380
Casandra, being
eventually consistent,

00:20:04.380 --> 00:20:07.620
you might say, hey, how do
you run transactional things

00:20:07.620 --> 00:20:08.520
in the old world?

00:20:08.520 --> 00:20:11.230
You might expect asset
type of transactions.

00:20:11.230 --> 00:20:13.980
But what we found is
that the Google network

00:20:13.980 --> 00:20:17.230
is so reliable, and so fast,
that even though Cassandra

00:20:17.230 --> 00:20:22.510
is eventually consistent, it
updates almost instantaneously.

00:20:22.510 --> 00:20:26.560
So we see very few problems with
the eventually consistent model

00:20:26.560 --> 00:20:27.060
there.

00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:29.100
MARK MANDEL: And I know Francesc
is going to ask this question,

00:20:29.100 --> 00:20:30.350
so I'm going to jump in on it.

00:20:30.350 --> 00:20:31.870
What languages do
you develop with?

00:20:31.870 --> 00:20:33.120
WILLIAM BONNELL: Yeah, not Go.

00:20:33.120 --> 00:20:34.280
Sorry, Francesc.

00:20:34.280 --> 00:20:35.240
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
OK, well, thank you

00:20:35.240 --> 00:20:36.448
so much for taking your time.

00:20:39.810 --> 00:20:42.162
WILLIAM BONNELL: We actually
have one of our pieces

00:20:42.162 --> 00:20:43.620
that you guys might
be interested--

00:20:43.620 --> 00:20:44.700
I can tell you a
little bit about that.

00:20:44.700 --> 00:20:46.500
But Java is our
primary platform.

00:20:46.500 --> 00:20:49.410
That's primarily because
we're a large enterprise,

00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:53.159
and at most enterprise you
would talk to, like Home Depot,

00:20:53.159 --> 00:20:54.450
it's probably going to be Java.

00:20:54.450 --> 00:20:55.825
Maybe you'd find
dot net as well.

00:20:55.825 --> 00:20:58.366
But those are going to be your
primary programming languages.

00:20:58.366 --> 00:21:00.230
And that was easier
for us to migrate to.

00:21:00.230 --> 00:21:04.500
We are, though, opening up the
ability for development teams

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:06.000
to use other
development platforms.

00:21:06.000 --> 00:21:07.230
But that's our primary one.

00:21:07.230 --> 00:21:08.646
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
And you mentioned

00:21:08.646 --> 00:21:10.680
you have a bunch of
different microservices.

00:21:10.680 --> 00:21:12.560
How do you communicate
across them?

00:21:12.560 --> 00:21:14.340
WILLIAM BONNELL: Yeah,
so we communicate.

00:21:14.340 --> 00:21:17.700
Actually, we communicate-- so
internal load balancer was not

00:21:17.700 --> 00:21:21.060
available when we decided
to go into Google.

00:21:21.060 --> 00:21:22.877
And so we actually
create a mechanism.

00:21:22.877 --> 00:21:24.960
And we've actually talked
to some other companies,

00:21:24.960 --> 00:21:26.130
did similar sort of things.

00:21:26.130 --> 00:21:28.410
We used Console for
service discovery.

00:21:28.410 --> 00:21:29.880
And so we built
that into our-- so

00:21:29.880 --> 00:21:33.240
if one service wants to call
another, we go through Console,

00:21:33.240 --> 00:21:36.420
basically look up the service,
and then make a direct call

00:21:36.420 --> 00:21:36.920
to it.

00:21:36.920 --> 00:21:37.836
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:21:37.836 --> 00:21:39.450
And RPC-wise, are
you using something

00:21:39.450 --> 00:21:41.415
really similar to gRPC?

00:21:41.415 --> 00:21:43.350
Did you build your own thing?

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:45.902
WILLIAM BONNELL: Yeah, we're
just using RESTful APIs.

00:21:45.902 --> 00:21:47.610
GRPC is something
we're looking at again.

00:21:47.610 --> 00:21:51.494
Availability of these
things hasn't been there.

00:21:51.494 --> 00:21:52.410
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:21:52.410 --> 00:21:53.584
It's also pretty new.

00:21:53.584 --> 00:21:54.500
WILLIAM BONNELL: Yeah.

00:21:54.500 --> 00:21:56.041
MARK MANDEL: So now
I'm also curious.

00:21:56.041 --> 00:21:58.380
We were talking about
that split between going

00:21:58.380 --> 00:22:00.990
from monolithic on-prem,
and then your microservices.

00:22:00.990 --> 00:22:03.669
How has that impacted
your CI/CD, your testing

00:22:03.669 --> 00:22:05.460
infrastructure, which
probably also impacts

00:22:05.460 --> 00:22:06.660
your liability, et
cetera, et cetera?

00:22:06.660 --> 00:22:07.743
WILLIAM BONNELL: Oh, yeah.

00:22:09.660 --> 00:22:11.280
It's like firehose.

00:22:11.280 --> 00:22:13.547
There's constant changes.

00:22:13.547 --> 00:22:14.380
That's a good thing.

00:22:14.380 --> 00:22:15.420
We're doing much
smaller changes,

00:22:15.420 --> 00:22:16.720
and we have problems with them.

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:19.261
We also built-- if you want to
talk about leveraging multiple

00:22:19.261 --> 00:22:22.110
zones, and multiple regions,
and leveraging Cloud Deployment

00:22:22.110 --> 00:22:24.630
Manager for doing those deploys,
and being able to roll them

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:26.887
out, and drain traffic
as we need to--

00:22:26.887 --> 00:22:28.470
so when we have
problems, the problems

00:22:28.470 --> 00:22:30.095
are much smaller than
they were before.

00:22:30.095 --> 00:22:32.610
If we're doing a big, weekly
release, and we had a problem,

00:22:32.610 --> 00:22:33.570
you'd feel it a lot more.

00:22:33.570 --> 00:22:34.950
And now, we're able
to do the changes.

00:22:34.950 --> 00:22:35.090
Yeah.

00:22:35.090 --> 00:22:36.750
You might have a little problem.

00:22:36.750 --> 00:22:38.340
So we're seeing that teams are--

00:22:38.340 --> 00:22:40.780
also, this is something
we're encouraging.

00:22:40.780 --> 00:22:44.040
And remember, I'm the
ops guy, if you will.

00:22:44.040 --> 00:22:46.500
So I might be the last
person you would expect.

00:22:46.500 --> 00:22:48.660
But we're encouraging
teams to spend less time,

00:22:48.660 --> 00:22:51.180
and not no time, but
less time on testing,

00:22:51.180 --> 00:22:54.090
and performance testing in
the lower lifecycle and a test

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:57.326
environment just because a lot
of time that's just simulated.

00:22:57.326 --> 00:22:58.950
Until you actually
get into production,

00:22:58.950 --> 00:23:01.400
get some traffic on it, get
some real customer traffic,

00:23:01.400 --> 00:23:03.180
you don't really know
what you're going to get.

00:23:03.180 --> 00:23:04.680
MARK MANDEL: And I'm guessing
then your rollback system

00:23:04.680 --> 00:23:05.820
is very simple and automated.

00:23:05.820 --> 00:23:07.290
So if you put
something out there,

00:23:07.290 --> 00:23:09.407
maybe you put 10% traffic
on it, see how it does,

00:23:09.407 --> 00:23:10.740
and you can always roll it back.

00:23:10.740 --> 00:23:11.010
WILLIAM BONNELL: That's right.

00:23:11.010 --> 00:23:12.360
That's been key to
everything we're

00:23:12.360 --> 00:23:14.151
doing from the very
beginning, is make sure

00:23:14.151 --> 00:23:16.010
all those are
completely automated.

00:23:16.010 --> 00:23:18.510
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
moving a little bit away

00:23:18.510 --> 00:23:23.550
from all the tech stuff, even
though it's super interesting,

00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:25.000
you were in the keynote.

00:23:25.000 --> 00:23:26.250
Tell us a little bit about it.

00:23:26.250 --> 00:23:27.630
What was the message?

00:23:27.630 --> 00:23:28.740
What happened up there?

00:23:28.740 --> 00:23:30.435
WILLIAM BONNELL: Sure, so
I wasn't in the keynote.

00:23:30.435 --> 00:23:32.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
I mean The Home Depot.

00:23:32.310 --> 00:23:33.730
MARK MANDEL: The Home Depot.

00:23:33.730 --> 00:23:35.820
WILLIAM BONNELL: Yeah,
so Paul Gaffney spoke.

00:23:35.820 --> 00:23:39.136
And one of the things
he talked about was--

00:23:39.136 --> 00:23:40.260
mentioned the Black Friday.

00:23:40.260 --> 00:23:41.760
That was a big deal for us.

00:23:41.760 --> 00:23:44.706
And for him to talk about
that-- as a retailer,

00:23:44.706 --> 00:23:47.080
you said you're in e-commerce
and how important that is--

00:23:47.080 --> 00:23:49.470
to put the trust
in Google, to be

00:23:49.470 --> 00:23:51.660
able to run those kind of
workloads, its volumes.

00:23:51.660 --> 00:23:55.590
And, really, we wanted to
highlight that for the world

00:23:55.590 --> 00:23:59.250
and say, hey, this is a platform
that we're finding highly

00:23:59.250 --> 00:24:03.300
reliable, excellent
performance, and then

00:24:03.300 --> 00:24:05.234
with the CRE engagement
on how to operate

00:24:05.234 --> 00:24:06.150
these things at scale.

00:24:06.150 --> 00:24:08.581
That was the message we wanted
to convey in the keynote.

00:24:08.581 --> 00:24:09.497
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:24:09.497 --> 00:24:12.600
And on top of that, there
were also two sessions.

00:24:12.600 --> 00:24:13.684
Do you remember the names?

00:24:13.684 --> 00:24:14.599
WILLIAM BONNELL: Sure.

00:24:14.599 --> 00:24:16.560
So the session I did,
I did with Luke Stone,

00:24:16.560 --> 00:24:19.631
who's on the CRE team, and
Mahesh from the network SRE

00:24:19.631 --> 00:24:20.130
team.

00:24:20.130 --> 00:24:22.230
It was called 24/7 Resiliency.

00:24:22.230 --> 00:24:23.509
So we looked at--

00:24:23.509 --> 00:24:24.300
it was Luke's idea.

00:24:24.300 --> 00:24:26.450
He did a great job
facilitating it.

00:24:26.450 --> 00:24:29.580
And then the idea
was thinking about--

00:24:29.580 --> 00:24:32.190
and actually, this is actually
a really important principle--

00:24:32.190 --> 00:24:33.970
is turtles all the way down.

00:24:33.970 --> 00:24:36.420
You've got the network,
the network SRE team.

00:24:36.420 --> 00:24:37.590
They run the network.

00:24:37.590 --> 00:24:38.580
The cloud team
doesn't really have

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:40.650
to know a whole lot about what
the network team is doing,

00:24:40.650 --> 00:24:42.816
provided the network team
is not causing any issues.

00:24:42.816 --> 00:24:45.120
And then the cloud,
the cloud SRE team

00:24:45.120 --> 00:24:46.282
is providing service to us.

00:24:46.282 --> 00:24:47.990
We don't really have
to know a whole lot.

00:24:47.990 --> 00:24:50.040
We don't care when you're
patching load balancers,

00:24:50.040 --> 00:24:51.960
and you're patching servers,
and those sorts of things.

00:24:51.960 --> 00:24:53.293
We don't really care about that.

00:24:53.293 --> 00:24:54.900
We don't need to
know about that.

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:56.900
And then you come to our
layer, the application,

00:24:56.900 --> 00:24:59.400
and we're running our
application, and our SRE team.

00:24:59.400 --> 00:25:01.890
And so it's really everybody
playing their position,

00:25:01.890 --> 00:25:02.912
and doing it well.

00:25:02.912 --> 00:25:03.870
MARK MANDEL: Excellent.

00:25:03.870 --> 00:25:05.900
And I believe you also had a
microservices talk as well?

00:25:05.900 --> 00:25:06.870
WILLIAM BONNELL: Yeah, we
had a microservices talk.

00:25:06.870 --> 00:25:09.061
On the third day, Robbie
[INAUDIBLE] talked with--

00:25:09.061 --> 00:25:10.560
and those names
just slipped my mind

00:25:10.560 --> 00:25:12.270
because it wasn't my session.

00:25:12.270 --> 00:25:16.020
But we talked about-- the
Googlers talked about security

00:25:16.020 --> 00:25:16.830
and load balancing.

00:25:16.830 --> 00:25:19.440
And then we reviewed
our microservices,

00:25:19.440 --> 00:25:20.550
also our lessons learned.

00:25:20.550 --> 00:25:23.550
And it was interesting because
those were the sessions

00:25:23.550 --> 00:25:24.480
your customers liked.

00:25:24.480 --> 00:25:26.910
Because I could see people like
taking pictures of the screen,

00:25:26.910 --> 00:25:27.740
the lessons learned one.

00:25:27.740 --> 00:25:29.156
I could see people
taking pictures

00:25:29.156 --> 00:25:30.630
of that, because
that's what really

00:25:30.630 --> 00:25:33.729
resonates with other people
thinking about using Google.

00:25:33.729 --> 00:25:36.270
FRANCESC CAMPOY: And for all
the people that we're not there,

00:25:36.270 --> 00:25:40.340
were not able to take
pictures of the screen,

00:25:40.340 --> 00:25:41.880
all of those talks
were recorded.

00:25:41.880 --> 00:25:43.740
And by the time this
episode comes out,

00:25:43.740 --> 00:25:45.450
they will be already online.

00:25:45.450 --> 00:25:48.379
So we'll have links to the
videos on the show notes.

00:25:48.379 --> 00:25:49.170
MARK MANDEL: Sweet.

00:25:49.170 --> 00:25:50.330
All right, cool.

00:25:50.330 --> 00:25:52.290
So we're running out of
time just a little bit.

00:25:52.290 --> 00:25:54.630
Is there anything else you
want to highlight, or plug,

00:25:54.630 --> 00:25:56.100
or anything we haven't
talked about yet that you

00:25:56.100 --> 00:25:57.130
think is really interesting?

00:25:57.130 --> 00:25:58.560
WILLIAM BONNELL: Well, we
talked a little bit about that

00:25:58.560 --> 00:25:59.143
prior to this.

00:25:59.143 --> 00:26:02.950
But the question around
DevOps versus SRE,

00:26:02.950 --> 00:26:05.877
and what's the difference,
at least from my perspective.

00:26:05.877 --> 00:26:06.960
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, sure.

00:26:06.960 --> 00:26:08.750
WILLIAM BONNELL: And so DevOp--

00:26:08.750 --> 00:26:10.560
and we actually had
something called

00:26:10.560 --> 00:26:13.144
DevOps within The Home Depot.

00:26:13.144 --> 00:26:15.060
But DevOps, and most
companies I've talked to,

00:26:15.060 --> 00:26:17.520
seems to be much
more CI/CD, build

00:26:17.520 --> 00:26:20.130
pipeline, automation-related,
and automating

00:26:20.130 --> 00:26:22.350
all those things, and the
testing and deployment

00:26:22.350 --> 00:26:23.460
of your application.

00:26:23.460 --> 00:26:25.260
Site reliability
engineering, with the way

00:26:25.260 --> 00:26:28.852
we've implemented it, also with
our relationship with Google,

00:26:28.852 --> 00:26:30.810
is much more around the
production environment,

00:26:30.810 --> 00:26:33.300
and automating and
engineering the production

00:26:33.300 --> 00:26:35.560
environment for high
availability, performance, et

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:36.060
cetera.

00:26:36.060 --> 00:26:38.930
So that's how we've implemented
it at The Home Depot.

00:26:38.930 --> 00:26:41.310
I will say-- I think you
asked the question earlier.

00:26:41.310 --> 00:26:43.829
Our level of-- everybody
we hire into our team,

00:26:43.829 --> 00:26:45.120
which by the way, we're hiring.

00:26:45.120 --> 00:26:46.577
Home Depot is hiring.

00:26:46.577 --> 00:26:47.660
MARK MANDEL: Good to know.

00:26:47.660 --> 00:26:49.740
WILLIAM BONNELL: Everybody
we hire into our team

00:26:49.740 --> 00:26:51.450
is going to have some
kind of automation

00:26:51.450 --> 00:26:54.120
background, generally Python.

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:57.120
Other programming languages are
fine because we're interacting

00:26:57.120 --> 00:26:59.310
with the Google APIs.

00:26:59.310 --> 00:27:01.470
But our level of
software engineering,

00:27:01.470 --> 00:27:04.252
I think comparable to Google
is probably not quite there.

00:27:04.252 --> 00:27:05.460
We want to grow it over time.

00:27:05.460 --> 00:27:06.376
We think about Google.

00:27:06.376 --> 00:27:10.020
They say, hey, 50% of their time
is actually building software.

00:27:10.020 --> 00:27:11.670
We're not quite
there yet, but that's

00:27:11.670 --> 00:27:13.849
something we're interested
in marching toward.

00:27:13.849 --> 00:27:14.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Amazing.

00:27:14.890 --> 00:27:17.430
So for people that
might have companies

00:27:17.430 --> 00:27:19.620
not maybe as big
as The Home Depot,

00:27:19.620 --> 00:27:21.870
but that are right
now on-premises

00:27:21.870 --> 00:27:24.540
and they're considering
moving to the cloud,

00:27:24.540 --> 00:27:27.420
what would be your
piece of advice?

00:27:27.420 --> 00:27:32.070
WILLIAM BONNELL:
So I would say one,

00:27:32.070 --> 00:27:34.714
regardless of where you
host, whether it's on a cloud

00:27:34.714 --> 00:27:36.130
that you have in
your data center,

00:27:36.130 --> 00:27:39.930
whether it's Google Cloud
platform, or a competitor,

00:27:39.930 --> 00:27:41.930
you need to build your
application cloud native,

00:27:41.930 --> 00:27:42.430
right--

00:27:42.430 --> 00:27:45.360
12-factor app, and
all the things that

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:47.610
go into building your
application in that way.

00:27:47.610 --> 00:27:49.510
You need to build
it in smaller pieces

00:27:49.510 --> 00:27:51.802
so you can do independent
development and deploy so you

00:27:51.802 --> 00:27:53.051
can get the business velocity.

00:27:53.051 --> 00:27:54.510
That would be the
number one thing.

00:27:54.510 --> 00:27:56.051
But the second thing
I would mention,

00:27:56.051 --> 00:27:58.350
especially when you're
building applications at scale,

00:27:58.350 --> 00:27:59.910
is the operational side of it.

00:27:59.910 --> 00:28:02.910
I think don't underestimate
the complexity of trying

00:28:02.910 --> 00:28:04.870
to run all-- especially
if you embrace

00:28:04.870 --> 00:28:07.110
a microservices architecture,
don't underestimate

00:28:07.110 --> 00:28:10.080
the complexity of running
all those pieces at four

00:28:10.080 --> 00:28:12.175
nines of availability.

00:28:12.175 --> 00:28:13.050
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:28:13.050 --> 00:28:15.810
