00:29:24.614 --> 00:29:25.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:27.680
I searched on Google.

00:29:27.680 --> 00:29:30.180
What's the difference between
Google Cloud Spanner and Cloud

00:29:30.180 --> 00:29:31.020
SQL?

00:29:31.020 --> 00:29:33.831
And I found a question
on Quora with the title,

00:29:33.831 --> 00:29:36.330
what's the difference between
Google Cloud Spanner and Cloud

00:29:36.330 --> 00:29:37.000
SQL?

00:29:37.000 --> 00:29:37.930
MARK MANDEL: How fortuitous.

00:29:37.930 --> 00:29:39.679
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It
is a very good answer.

00:29:39.679 --> 00:29:41.605
So we'll definitely link to it.

00:29:41.605 --> 00:29:43.730
I'm going to try to say
the name of the person that

00:29:43.730 --> 00:29:44.920
wrote the answer.

00:29:44.920 --> 00:29:47.472
It's [? Zanco Houdicjeck. ?]

00:29:47.472 --> 00:29:48.930
MARK MANDEL: That
seems good to me.

00:29:48.930 --> 00:29:50.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
[? Houdicjheck, ?] I think.

00:29:50.090 --> 00:29:51.060
MARK MANDEL: Sure, sure.

00:29:51.060 --> 00:29:53.226
FRANCESC CAMPOY: If someone
knows how to pronounce--

00:29:53.226 --> 00:29:55.470
I'd say that probably this
is some Slavic language.

00:29:55.470 --> 00:29:57.360
If anyone knows how
to pronounce it,

00:29:57.360 --> 00:29:59.640
I'd love to be
corrected about this.

00:29:59.640 --> 00:30:01.950
[? Zanco ?] says
the main difference

00:30:01.950 --> 00:30:07.050
is that Spanner is horizontally
scalable and Cloud SQL is not.

00:30:07.050 --> 00:30:08.700
What does that actually mean?

00:30:08.700 --> 00:30:12.360
What it means is that Cloud
SQL is vertically scalable,

00:30:12.360 --> 00:30:17.535
which means that if your server
is getting too small for you,

00:30:17.535 --> 00:30:21.030
you can make that server bigger.

00:30:21.030 --> 00:30:24.030
So you can add disk, you can
add memory, you can add CPUs,

00:30:24.030 --> 00:30:25.845
but you cannot
really add servers.

00:30:25.845 --> 00:30:29.310
MARK MANDEL: Yep, so once
you hit that vertical limit,

00:30:29.310 --> 00:30:30.060
you're stuck.

00:30:30.060 --> 00:30:31.019
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:30:31.019 --> 00:30:33.060
The whole thing is that
if you want more servers,

00:30:33.060 --> 00:30:34.260
you could actually do it.

00:30:34.260 --> 00:30:36.420
But then you start
basically paying

00:30:36.420 --> 00:30:39.780
for the extra cost
of those SQL servers

00:30:39.780 --> 00:30:41.580
connecting to each other.

00:30:41.580 --> 00:30:43.624
And it doesn't scale that well.

00:30:43.624 --> 00:30:45.540
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, you
could do read replicas.

00:30:45.540 --> 00:30:47.780
But you, obviously,
doing write replicas--

00:30:47.780 --> 00:30:49.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
for read replicas-- yeah,

00:30:49.780 --> 00:30:52.470
read replicas, that works.

00:30:52.470 --> 00:30:55.120
For write replicas, it
doesn't really work.

00:30:55.120 --> 00:30:57.750
Now, at Spanner, it
allows you to do this.

00:30:57.750 --> 00:30:59.800
Basically, for every
note that you add,

00:30:59.800 --> 00:31:02.250
you get extra throughput.

00:31:02.250 --> 00:31:05.160
So you get extra
memory, extra CPUs,

00:31:05.160 --> 00:31:07.240
extra everything, but
also, extra throughput,

00:31:07.240 --> 00:31:08.781
which is what you
really care about--

00:31:08.781 --> 00:31:11.580
extra throughput both
for reads and writes.

00:31:11.580 --> 00:31:12.570
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:14.278
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
with that, basically,

00:31:14.278 --> 00:31:17.070
if you have a cluster
of [INAUDIBLE] notes,

00:31:17.070 --> 00:31:18.600
and at some point
you're like, oh,

00:31:18.600 --> 00:31:21.270
we're very close
to the capacity--

00:31:21.270 --> 00:31:24.280
I want to scale this
up-- you just add a note.

00:31:24.280 --> 00:31:25.350
That's it.

00:31:25.350 --> 00:31:27.920
You don't need to do anything
extra, which is really cool.

00:31:27.920 --> 00:31:31.260
Now, you need to take into
account that, yes, it allows

00:31:31.260 --> 00:31:33.570
you-- so Spanner allows
you to basically do

00:31:33.570 --> 00:31:35.105
the same cool things
that you can do

00:31:35.105 --> 00:31:37.037
with SQL for select statements.

00:31:37.037 --> 00:31:39.370
So when you're reading, you
can do pretty much the same.

00:31:39.370 --> 00:31:42.439
And when you're
writing too though,

00:31:42.439 --> 00:31:43.980
the semantics are
slightly different.

00:31:43.980 --> 00:31:47.160
You cannot use SQL, for
now at least, to write.

00:31:47.160 --> 00:31:50.940
In Spanner, you're going to need
to use the gRPC or REST APIs.

00:31:50.940 --> 00:31:53.890
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, but
those are idiomatic APIs

00:31:53.890 --> 00:31:55.140
for each language that use it.

00:31:55.140 --> 00:31:56.660
So it's not like you're
writing an insert,

00:31:56.660 --> 00:31:58.650
like command rather than
writing an insert statement.

00:31:58.650 --> 00:31:59.340
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, so basically

00:31:59.340 --> 00:32:00.870
the thing to take
into account is

00:32:00.870 --> 00:32:03.090
that if you have something
brewing with Cloud SQL,

00:32:03.090 --> 00:32:04.650
and you want to
migrate to Spanner--

00:32:04.650 --> 00:32:05.530
MARK MANDEL: There's
a bit of work.

00:32:05.530 --> 00:32:07.440
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --you may need
to change that a little bit,

00:32:07.440 --> 00:32:08.190
not for the reads.

00:32:08.190 --> 00:32:10.074
For the writes, you
may need to change it--

00:32:10.074 --> 00:32:11.490
so something to
take into account.

00:32:11.490 --> 00:32:12.210
MARK MANDEL: Now,
it's also worth

00:32:12.210 --> 00:32:14.160
noting Cloud Spanner is
going to be a bit more

00:32:14.160 --> 00:32:16.230
expensive than Cloud SQL.

00:32:16.230 --> 00:32:19.110
So I figure it almost
comes down to how much

00:32:19.110 --> 00:32:21.960
data do you expect to have,
or how much data do you have,

00:32:21.960 --> 00:32:24.720
and what sort of read and
write throughput do you need?

00:32:24.720 --> 00:32:25.860
If maybe you have--

00:32:25.860 --> 00:32:27.900
I don't know-- 100,000
records or so, maybe you

00:32:27.900 --> 00:32:29.392
keep that all in Cloud SQL.

00:32:29.392 --> 00:32:31.350
If you start getting into
millions or billions,

00:32:31.350 --> 00:32:33.350
and you need to be able
to have access to those,

00:32:33.350 --> 00:32:35.940
and interact with them
in a transactional way,

00:32:35.940 --> 00:32:39.097
in a high read/write
throughput way, then maybe then

00:32:39.097 --> 00:32:41.430
that's when you start looking
at something like spanner.

00:32:41.430 --> 00:32:44.370
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So, yeah, just
to give you an idea of node per

00:32:44.370 --> 00:32:49.530
hour cost, it's $0.90,
which is not that much.

00:32:49.530 --> 00:32:53.610
But again, it can go up to
$600 or something like that,

00:32:53.610 --> 00:32:55.000
I think, per month.

00:32:55.000 --> 00:32:57.240
So Cloud SQL, you
can have definitely

00:32:57.240 --> 00:32:58.650
way, way cheaper than that.

00:32:58.650 --> 00:33:00.750
Now, the difference
is that at some point,

00:33:00.750 --> 00:33:03.180
you cannot make
Cloud SQL go faster.

00:33:03.180 --> 00:33:05.140
Spanner, you can always
make it go faster.

